WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.532
I need the Department of Higher Education.

00:00:02.692 --> 00:00:03.609
Tab to About.

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:04.498
Down arrow.

00:00:04.658 --> 00:00:05.737
Where did the link go?

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:09.009
Down arrow leaves About focused.

00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:10.789
The link underneath stays hidden.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.540
On OpenSeaMap, the keyboard skips View.

00:00:17.143 --> 00:00:19.193
They press Tab to move through the page.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.664
At Munich Airport, listen to the previous
slide control.

00:00:28.824 --> 00:00:29.270
[Reading voice] Button.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.228
On ExtremeTerrain, the Details link is faint.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.838
I turn on Curb Cut.

00:00:37.998 --> 00:00:38.809
Same menu.

00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:39.932
Same keyboard.

00:00:41.912 --> 00:00:42.609
There it is.

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:43.635
The DHE.

00:00:44.702 --> 00:00:46.210
And Enter takes you to the page.

00:00:47.034 --> 00:00:50.659
On Munich Airport, Curb Cut names it:
previous slide.

00:00:50.993 --> 00:00:52.177
[Reading voice] Vorherige Folie.

00:00:54.106 --> 00:00:57.156
Curb Cut nudges the colour
just enough to be readable,

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:57.777
and measures it.

00:00:59.139 --> 00:01:02.614
And on pages we've never fixed,
there's an experiment.

00:01:02.614 --> 00:01:05.564
Jev, a fast decision model from TypeSafe,

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:08.283
names unlabelled buttons live,
in about a second.

00:01:08.405 --> 00:01:11.730
On real sites,
13 of its 15 names were right.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.542
When it isn't sure, it stays quiet.

00:01:13.903 --> 00:01:17.978
On ColorHexa, try opening this blue swatch
using only the keyboard.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:23.958
Kane, an AI testing agent, tries the task
the way a disabled person would.

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:24.609
It fails.

00:01:26.144 --> 00:01:27.869
Kane tries again with a mouse.

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:29.037
The same task works.

00:01:29.206 --> 00:01:31.352
So the website is broken, not the task.

00:01:32.973 --> 00:01:35.865
Here’s a failure that went straight back to
the coding agent.

00:01:36.191 --> 00:01:40.886
Its identifier travels with the assertion
into the coding agent’s repair record.

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:44.377
Let the button take focus,
and let Enter activate it.

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.158
Kane runs the task again with the fix,
and it passes.

00:01:48.158 --> 00:01:49.451
The recording is the proof.

00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:56.583
Back on the original tab,
Shift+Tab reaches the swatch.

00:01:57.003 --> 00:01:58.269
Enter opens the picker.

00:02:00.866 --> 00:02:04.246
If something else gets in the
way, open Report a barrier.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.863
One shortcut reports the barrier.

00:02:07.166 --> 00:02:09.361
Grok writes the fix
in sixteen seconds.

00:02:09.666 --> 00:02:12.525
Kane tries it. Without the fix, the
keyboard fails.

00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:13.800
The mouse works.

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:16.840
With the fix, the keyboard passes.

00:02:17.166 --> 00:02:20.568
Minutes later, after a recheck,
the fix ships in the extension.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.780
Now the same eight Tabs open View.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.479
Sea Marks is right there.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.500
Today, 106 fixes are live
in the extension.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.813
62 of them proven by Kane.

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:34.173
A developer can put that
check into Claude Code, too.

00:02:34.173 --> 00:02:36.309
Here it runs when Claude tries to finish.

00:02:36.729 --> 00:02:39.443
The first time, the button
still skips the keyboard.

00:02:39.443 --> 00:02:42.404
The check fails and the Stop
hook sends the work back.

00:02:43.753 --> 00:02:46.593
The developer fixes the
button in the site itself.

00:02:46.593 --> 00:02:48.169
Claude reaches the end again.

00:02:48.589 --> 00:02:50.753
This time Kane can add the headphones.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:52.856
The check passes and Claude can finish.

00:02:54.034 --> 00:02:56.484
And every fix can go back to the site owner:

00:02:56.484 --> 00:02:59.934
the exact change,
with Kane's recording as proof.

00:03:00.153 --> 00:03:04.033
And when a site owner makes a repair,
the browser should get out of the way.

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:07.470
Kane tries the task with the
extension's fix switched off.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.393
The button works on its own.

00:03:09.393 --> 00:03:11.170
Curb Cut retires the fix.

00:03:12.653 --> 00:03:17.261
The next person who comes to buy those headphones
can use the keyboard from the start.

00:03:19.213 --> 00:03:20.844
Install Curb Cut for Chrome.
